<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chichester</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chichester is a cathedral city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It began as the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noviomagus-reginorum">Noviomagus Reginorum</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regni">Regni</a>, where an inscription names the client king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togidubnus">Togidubnus</a>. The bishopric moved there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selsey">Selsey</a> in 1075, and its cathedral dates largely from the 12th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chichester.jpg" alt="Chichester" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Market_Cross_Chichester_1026826_20230815_0001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
